  {
   "id": "10h3i8l-BwAJ",
   "from": "Finn Lindgren",
   "author": "afb003e064114795",
   "date": "2016-07-23T12:58:07Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Poisson Likelihood with non-integer response",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"auto\"><div>The document</div><div>  <a href=\"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/pscl/vignettes/countreg.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/pscl/vignettes/countreg.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792444839959000&amp;usg=AOvVaw32TqutYKyOTgTvBBW6II83\">https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/pscl/vignettes/countreg.pdf</a></div><div>has this to say about quasi-Poisson:</div><div>&quot;<span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:CMR10\">Another way of dealing with over-dispersion is to use the mean regression function and the\r\nvariance function from the Poisson GLM but to leave the dispersion parameter </span><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:CMMI10\">φ </span><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:CMR10\">unrestricted.\r\nThus, </span><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:CMMI10\">φ </span><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:CMR10\">is not assumed to be fixed at 1 but is estimated from the data. This strategy leads\r\nto the same coefficient estimates as the standard Poisson model but inference is adjusted for\r\nover-dispersion. Consequently, both models (quasi-Poisson and sandwich-adjusted Poisson)\r\nadopt the estimating function view of the Poisson model and do </span><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:CMTI10\">not </span><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:CMR10\">correspond to models\r\nwith fully specified likelihoods.&quot;</span></div><div><br>Finn</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"auto\"><div><br>On 23 Jul 2016, at 13:46, INLA help &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">he...@r-inla.org</a>&gt; wrote:<br><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"auto\"><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><span>On Sat, 2016-07-23 at 05:37 -0700, AJ wrote:</span><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"><span>Hello,</span><br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\"><span></span><br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\"><span>Is it possible to fit a Poisson model to non-integer data in INLA</span><br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\"><span>(similar to what the quasipoisson family does in glm)?</span><br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\"><span> </span><br></blockquote><span></span><br><span>no. </span><br><span></span><br><span>what would the expression for that likelihood be in that case?</span><br><span></span><br><span></span><br><span>-- </span><br><span>Håvard Rue</span><br><span><a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">he...@r-inla.org</a></span><br><span></span><br></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"auto\"><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><span>-- </span><br><span>You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;R-inla discussion group&quot; group.</span><br><span>To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">r-inla-discussion...@googlegroups.com</a>.</span><br><span>To post to this group, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">r-inla-disc...@googlegroups.com</a>.</span><br><span>Visit this group at <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792444839959000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0SzXcdlFanPXOex-BOe9x4\">https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group</a>.</span><br><span>For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792444839959000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3c7ik7DcYN9N64ONvvvtPv\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.</span><br></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
