  {
   "id": "ZrlqwrOBBwAJ",
   "from": "AJ",
   "author": "996fe8a741b3deb6",
   "date": "2016-07-23T13:51:32Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Poisson Likelihood with non-integer response",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">what would the expression for that likelihood be in that case?\r\n<br>\r\n<br></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Thanks for your reply,<br><br>Let S={y_1, y_2, ...} be a countable set of non-negative numbers (not necessary integers) such that y_i \\to \\infty as i \\to \\infty. Then for any \\lambda &gt; 0,<br>   C exp( -\\lambda + y_i \\log\\lambda ) / \\Gamma(1 + y_i),   i=1,2, ...<br>defines a distribution (probability mass function) on S, where<br>  C = exp(-\\lambda) \\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty}  \\exp( y_i \\log\\lambda ) / \\Gamma(1 + y_i) &lt; \\infty<br>is the normalizing factor. Thus the likelihood is proportional to the likelihood of an ordinary Poisson distribution. <br>This means that the same inference is obtained by using the Poisson likelihood for such data.<br><br>In the glm function, family=poisson with non-integer response returns warnings because of calling the Poisson density (mass) function with non-integer values.<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">6.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">7.8</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4.6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3.4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2.4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.8</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> glm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> family</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">poisson</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Call</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  glm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">formula </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> family </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> poisson</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Coefficients</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Intercept</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">            x  <br>    </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.2077</span><span style=\"color:#000\">       </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1991</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  <br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Degrees</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> of </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Freedom</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">7</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Total</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">e</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Null</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Residual</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Null</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Deviance</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">        </span><span style=\"color:#066\">5.026</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Residual</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Deviance</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.474</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     AIC</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Inf</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Warning</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> messages</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">integer x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.400000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">integer x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.200000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">integer x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3.100000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">integer x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3.200000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">integer x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.500000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">integer x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2.400000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">7</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">integer x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.800000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#066\">8</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">integer x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.100000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br><br>But with family=quasipoisson, the same results are obtained with no warnings.<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> glm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> family</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">quasipoisson</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Call</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  glm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">formula </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> family </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> quasipoisson</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Coefficients</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Intercept</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">            x  <br>    </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.2077</span><span style=\"color:#000\">       </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1991</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  <br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Degrees</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> of </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Freedom</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">7</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Total</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">e</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Null</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Residual</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Null</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Deviance</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">        </span><span style=\"color:#066\">5.026</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Residual</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Deviance</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.474</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     AIC</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> NA</span></div></code></div><br>So, I guess it depends on the way the Poisson likelihood is implemented in INLA. It seams to work fine.<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> stk </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> effects</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> res </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> family</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;poisson&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stack</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stk</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> res$summary</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">fixed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>                  mean        sd   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.025quant</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5quant</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.975quant</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Intercept</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1903892</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5603239</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.379172167</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1587073</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.8251839</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>x            </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1990556</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1053645</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.005815719</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1983585</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.4076384</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>How does INLA treat non-integer values in the Poisson likelihood? <br>Does it simply calculate the likelihood at the data points regardless of their type (integer or non-integer)?<br><br>Best regards,<br><br>Abdollah<br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "DmaJzB2CBwAJ",
   "from": "INLA help",
   "author": "3eab71733027a24a",
   "date": "2016-07-23T13:59:08Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Poisson Likelihood with non-integer response",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On Sat, 2016-07-23 at 06:51 -0700, AJ wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; &gt; what would the expression for that likelihood be in that case? \r<br>&gt; &gt; \r<br>&gt; Thanks for your reply,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Let S={y_1, y_2, ...} be a countable set of non-negative numbers (not\r<br>&gt; necessary integers) such that y_i \\to \\infty as i \\to \\infty. Then\r<br>&gt; for any \\lambda &gt; 0,\r<br>&gt;    C exp( -\\lambda + y_i \\log\\lambda ) / \\Gamma(1 + y_i),   i=1,2,\r<br>&gt; ...\r<br>&gt; defines a distribution (probability mass function) on S, where\r<br>&gt;   C = exp(-\\lambda) \\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty}  \\exp( y_i \\log\\lambda ) /\r<br>&gt; \\Gamma(1 + y_i) &lt; \\infty\r<br>&gt; is the normalizing factor. Thus the likelihood is proportional to the\r<br>&gt; likelihood of an ordinary Poisson distribution. \r<br>&gt; This means that the same inference is obtained by using the Poisson\r<br>&gt; likelihood for such data.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "so to compute the normalizing constant, we&#39;ll need to know all the\r<br>&#39;y_i&#39; values it _could take ? \r<br>-- \r<br>Håvard Rue\r<br><a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">he...@r-inla.org</a>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "eyjIQSWEBwAJ",
   "from": "AJ",
   "author": "996fe8a741b3deb6",
   "date": "2016-07-23T14:36:19Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Poisson Likelihood with non-integer response",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">so to compute the normalizing constant, we&#39;ll need to know all the\r\n<br>&#39;y_i&#39; values it _could take ? \r\n<br></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>Yes, for non-integer data the normalizing constant &#39;C&#39; is not easy (if \r\nnot impossible) to calculate. But for parameter estimation, the \r\nnormalizing constant cancels out in the posterior density and therefore \r\nwe do not need to compute it. <br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "QereMG2XBwAJ",
   "from": "INLA help",
   "author": "3eab71733027a24a",
   "date": "2016-07-23T20:29:38Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Poisson Likelihood with non-integer response",
   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "On Sat, 2016-07-23 at 07:36 -0700, AJ wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; &gt; so to compute the normalizing constant, we&#39;ll need to know all the \r<br>&gt; &gt; &#39;y_i&#39; values it _could take ?  \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Yes, for non-integer data the normalizing constant &#39;C&#39; is not easy\r<br>&gt; (if not impossible) to calculate. But for parameter estimation, the\r<br>&gt; normalizing constant cancels out in the posterior density and\r<br>&gt; therefore we do not need to compute it. \r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "it does depend on \\lambda... \r<br>-- \r<br>Håvard Rue\r<br><a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">he...@r-inla.org</a>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "smTmbm2YBwAJ",
   "from": "Finn Lindgren",
   "author": "afb003e064114795",
   "date": "2016-07-23T20:47:59Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Poisson Likelihood with non-integer response",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt;&gt; Yes, for non-integer data the normalizing constant &#39;C&#39; is not easy\r<br>&gt;&gt; (if not impossible) to calculate. But for parameter estimation, the\r<br>&gt;&gt; normalizing constant cancels out in the posterior density and\r<br>&gt;&gt; therefore we do not need to compute it. \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; it does depend on \\lambda... \r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Precisely.  Like they said in the document I referenced, the pseudo-poisson &quot;model&quot; (it appears to be the same one discussed here) used by the glm packages is based on estimating equations, and doesn&#39;t have a likelihood model. One can define one in the way discussed in this thread, and that may mean something similar, but as Håvard says, the normalization depends on \\lambda, so it only cancels out for a fixed lambda, and not for the whole posterior range of kappa.\r<br>\r<br>Finn\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; -- \r<br>&gt; Håvard Rue\r<br>&gt; <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">he...@r-inla.org</a>\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; -- \r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;R-inla discussion group&quot; group.\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">r-inla-discussion...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; To post to this group, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">r-inla-disc...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; Visit this group at <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792444839983000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2IFmHaxxx88RrY6eARsCa9\">https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group</a>.\r<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792444839983000&amp;usg=AOvVaw29VnXgjyOihqk-ZV5LzJ13\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "9xxZCXabBwAJ",
   "from": "AJ",
   "author": "996fe8a741b3deb6",
   "date": "2016-07-23T21:43:35Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Poisson Likelihood with non-integer response",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">&gt; it does depend on \\lambda... \r\n<br></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>That is right. Sorry, my mistake. <br><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">Precisely.  Like they said in the document I referenced, the pseudo-poisson &quot;model&quot; (it appears to be the same one discussed here) used by the glm packages is based on estimating equations, and doesn&#39;t have a likelihood model. One can define one in the way discussed in this thread, and that may mean something similar, but as Håvard says, the normalization depends on \\lambda, so it only cancels out for a fixed lambda, and not for the whole posterior range of kappa.\r\n<br>\r\n<br></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>Yes, the estimation for the quasipoisson family in glm is based on a generalized estimation equation which happens to coincide with the likelihood (score = 0) equation of the Poisson model. Thank you for the detailed answer. In fact the model considered above belongs to an over-dispersed exponential family of distributions as discussed in the following reference:<br><br><div>Gelfand, A. E., &amp; Dalal, S. R. (1990). A note on overdispersed exponential families. <i>Biometrika</i>, <i>77</i>(1), 55-64.</div><br> </div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
